      <h1 class="section-heading blog-article-heading" id="articleHeading">
        Back To “Basics”: How Airlines and Their Evolving Loyalty Programs Are Competing for Your Dollars in 2027
      </h1>

      <p class="blog-article-meta">
        Airline Comparisons · <strong>Review draft</strong> · Prepared
        <time datetime="2026-10-15">October 15, 2026</time>
      </p>

      <img
        class="blog-article-photo"
        src="../images/blog/backtobasics.jpg"
        alt=""
        decoding="async"
      >

      <div class="blog-article-content">
        <p>Southwest Airlines announced sweeping changes to its Rapid Rewards loyalty program today, October 15, 2026. Frontier has also announced changes that will require loyal travelers to buy a bundle to unlock certain seating benefits. The question remains: which airline comes out on top when you’re looking for value in your air travel?</p>

        <p>I can’t say I’m surprised by Southwest’s direction. To me, Southwest lost much of its value edge as it pushed toward a more conventional airline model amid pressure from activist investor Elliott. Benefits loyal customers expected and “luved” have changed: two free checked bags are no longer universal, and open seating has given way to assigned seats. Some benefits now depend on your fare, status or credit card. <a href="https://investors.southwest.com/sec-filings/all-sec-filings/content/0001193125-24-242286/d899498dex101.htm">Southwest’s agreement with Elliott</a> provides the corporate backdrop.</p>

        <p>With the push to be like the “Big Guys” came a push toward a more premium experience: Extra Legroom seats, connectivity and cabin upgrades. In-seat USB power on equipped Southwest aircraft is another small comfort that matters when you’re traveling. It is not something I would count on for every flight. <a href="https://mobile.southwest.com/inflight-entertainment-portal/">Southwest’s onboard technology</a>.</p>

        <p>Southwest has also announced new status tiers, Southwest Icon Lounges expected to begin opening in late 2027, and a new premium credit card. Those lounges are a future promise, not a January travel benefit. <a href="https://www.southwest.com/rapid-rewards/2027/whats-changing">Southwest’s announced plans</a>.</p>

        <p>But in chasing a more premium identity, Southwest hasn’t erased its entire customer experience. A carry-on remains included. Snacks and nonalcoholic drinks are included on eligible flights. And <strong>Basic still earns rewards and allows applicable elite benefits</strong>. See <a href="https://www.southwest.com/airfare-types-benefits/">Southwest’s fare benefits</a>. That last part matters when another airline asks you to pay extra just to use the seating perks your loyalty was supposed to unlock.</p>

        <h2 class="blog-article-subheading">Frontier’s loyalty squeeze</h2>

        <p>Frontier faces a familiar choice: compete on a genuinely low total price, or ask its already loyal customers to spend more for the experience they thought they had earned. You can probably guess how I feel about that.</p>

        <p>Beyond the announced changes, my own searches have left me feeling squeezed:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Fewer seats I can use my perks on.</strong> I’ve seen row 3 offered as UpFront Plus rather than as premium seating. On those seat maps, that means fewer premium seats available under my current elite benefits. I’m describing what I saw, not claiming that every aircraft has the same layout.</li>
          <li><strong>Discount Den looked practically worthless in my sample.</strong> Across more than a dozen flights on multiple routes and dates, the savings I observed were a whopping <em>one dollar</em>. That is my sample, not a guarantee of what another traveler will find.</li>
          <li><strong>GoWild! bundle prices deserve a close look.</strong> In my searches, the cash price of an Economy bundle attached to a GoWild! fare was higher than the bundle price shown with other fare types. That observation is not proof of a universal pricing rule—but it is a reason to compare the final checkout price.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>It almost feels as if someone said, “They’re loyal. Surely they’ll pay us more.” That’s how the pricing lands with me, not a claim about a conversation inside Frontier.</p>

        <p>Does Frontier still present great value at times? <strong>Yes!</strong> But at some point you have to ask when the travel experience outweighs the savings. In my experience, app errors and glitches make the premium pitch harder to swallow. A polished brand does not fix a frustrating trip.</p>

        <h2 class="blog-article-subheading">Let’s compare: Denver to Las Vegas</h2>
        <p>Below are the <strong>sample minimum Basic fares I found for nonstop DEN–LAS travel during January 2027</strong>. These are observations from my search, not monthly averages, guaranteed prices or live quotes. The lowest fare on each airline may be on a different date or flight.</p>

