        <h2 class="blog-article-subheading">The fine print that changes the math</h2>
        <p id="note-cancel"><strong>Cancellation:</strong> Southwest Basic is nonrefundable. Cancel at least ten minutes before the original scheduled departure to preserve any eligible credit; Basic credits normally expire six months from original booking, potentially earlier when older funds are used. No fee does not mean cash back. Frontier’s published $149 Basic cancellation fee applies to bookings made on or after September 25, 2026, subject to exceptions. If that fee exceeds the ticket’s value, no credit remains; don’t treat it as a separate $149 bill on top of a $79 fare. Qualifying 24-hour cancellations and airline-caused disruptions can have different refund rules. See <a href="https://support.southwest.com/helpcenter/article/basic-fare">Southwest Basic terms</a>, <a href="https://support.southwest.com/helpcenter/article/flight-credits">credit expiration</a> and <a href="https://faq.flyfrontier.com/help/can-i-cancel-my-reservation">Frontier cancellation terms</a>. Frontier’s general FAQ still lists eligible elite waivers; its announced January rules remove the blanket elite waiver, so check the rules attached to your booking.</p>

        <p id="note-bags"><strong>Status still counts on Frontier Basic.</strong> Under the announced rules, Gold includes a carry-on for the member; Platinum includes carry-ons and one checked bag per traveler on the booking; Diamond includes carry-ons and two checked bags per traveler. Silver does not include a carry-on. Seat upgrades require a bundle, with availability-dependent UpFront Plus/First Class windows of 4, 8, 18 and 24 hours for Silver, Gold, Platinum and Diamond, respectively. That is a narrower criticism than saying Basic loses every elite perk. <a href="https://www.flyfrontier.com/frontiermiles/terms-and-conditions/">Frontier Miles terms</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>Southwest tiers are not interchangeable.</strong> A-List becomes Silver, A-List Preferred becomes Gold, and Platinum is new on January 1. The seating distinctions in the table matter: Silver’s Extra Legroom access starts within 48 hours of departure, rather than being an automatic check-in upgrade. Every seat benefit remains subject to availability. <a href="https://www.southwest.com/rapid-rewards/2027/">Southwest’s 2027 tier benefits</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>Points are earned on eligible spend.</strong> The table compares the base-member earning rates without a credit card. Multiplying the entire displayed fare by 2 or 1 would overstate earnings if it includes taxes or other ineligible charges. Without the fare breakdowns, I’m not assigning exact point totals. See <a href="https://www.southwest.com/rapid-rewards/2027/">Southwest’s earning rules</a> and <a href="https://www.flyfrontier.com/frontiermiles/terms-and-conditions/">Frontier’s eligible-purchase rules</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>Onboard amenities:</strong> Southwest refreshments are subject to flight eligibility and service conditions; free Wi-Fi requires Rapid Rewards membership and availability. Frontier currently sells refreshments and lists no Wi-Fi or electrical outlets. I’ve left unverified menu prices out of the comparison. See <a href="https://www.southwest.com/food-and-beverage/">Southwest’s menu</a>, <a href="https://www.southwest.com/rapid-rewards/2027/whats-changing">Wi-Fi terms</a> and <a href="https://faq.flyfrontier.com/help/in-flight">Frontier’s inflight information</a>.</p>

        <h2 class="blog-article-subheading">When does the cheaper fare stop being the better deal?</h2>
        <p>The sample starts with a $59 gap. If you need to buy Frontier’s carry-on at the $69 price I observed, the arithmetic becomes <strong>$79 + $69 = $148</strong>—$10 above Southwest’s $138 sample fare, before buying a snack or drink. That is illustrative math using separate sample minimums, not a matched-flight quote or a complete bundle comparison. <a href="https://www.flyfrontier.com/travel/travel-info/bag-options/?mobile=true#bag-prices-for-new-trip">Frontier’s Bag Price Checker</a>.</p>

        <p>If your Frontier status or another benefit already covers the bag—or you travel with only a personal item—the $69 addition does not belong in your calculation. Frontier may still win comfortably. That is why the final price for <em>your</em> trip matters more than either airline’s headline fare.</p>

        <p>For me, loyalty should still buy something when I choose the cheapest fare. Frontier can absolutely win my business with a low enough total price. But if I have to pay more to unlock the seating perks I earned, while Southwest includes more of the experience I actually use, the decision gets a lot less “Basic.” Compare the whole trip, count the benefits you will really use, and make the airline earn your dollars—not just your loyalty.</p>

